<article class="env-lemma" id="0C3U">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C3U">Lemma <span data-tag="0C3U">33.39.4</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a reduced Nagata local ring of dimension $1$. The $\delta $-invariant of $A$ is $0$ if and only if $A$ is a discrete valuation ring. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $A$ is a discrete valuation ring, then $A$ is normal and the ring $A'$ is equal to $A$. Conversely, if the $\delta $-invariant of $A$ is $0$, then $A$ is integrally closed in its total ring of fractions which implies that $A$ is normal (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/030C" data-tag="030C">10.37.16</a>) and this forces $A$ to be a discrete valuation ring by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00PD" data-tag="00PD">10.119.7</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>